    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d145">

                <pb facs="0238" n="222" xml:id="pg222"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">501.A Summaries/11–2949: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The United States Representative at the United
                        Nations</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Austin</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg></opener>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">New York</hi></placeName>, <date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1949-11-29T00:45:00-05:00">November 29,
                        1949—12:45 a. m.</date>
                </dateline>
                <p>1378.</p>
                <p>. . . . . . .</p>
                <p rendition="#center">
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Committee</hi> 1 (339<hi rendition="#small-caps">th
                        Meeting</hi>)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d145-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p rendition="#center">
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">summary</hi>
                </p>
                <p>The joint US-Australia-Mexico-Pakistan-Philippines resolution on “Promotion of
                    the Stability of International Relations in the Far East” (A/C.1/552) was
                    presented by Jessup in Committee 1 Nov. 28. In his accompanying statement,
                    Jessup suggested referral to the ICJ of the question of violations of the
                    Sino-Soviet Treaty.</p>
                <p>Australia, Greece, Venezuela and Belgium also spoke, the last-named taking a
                    strong position and reserving judgment as between the two resolutions. Tsiang
                    (China) said the joint resolution covered the last point of his resolution, and
                    insisted on a decision on the other three points. The Committee voted, on
                    Brazil’s motion, to adjourn pending completion of the “essentials of peace” item
                    in Plenary.</p>
                <p>. . . . . . .</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Austin</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d145-f1" n="1" xml:id="d145-f1-content">
